<article class="env-lemma" id="0FCK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FCK">Lemma <span data-tag="0FCK">12.9.7</span> <span class="named">(Jordan-Hölder)</span>.</a>  Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let $A$ be an object of $\mathcal{A}$ satisfying the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0FCJ" data-tag="0FCJ">12.9.6</a>. Given two filtrations </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \subset A_1 \subset A_2 \subset \ldots \subset A_ n = A \quad \text{and}\quad 0 \subset B_1 \subset B_2 \subset \ldots \subset B_ m = A  \]
</div>
  <p> with $S_ i = A_ i/A_{i - 1}$ and $T_ j = B_ j/B_{j - 1}$ simple objects we have $n = m$ and there exists a permutation $\sigma $ of $\{ 1, \ldots , n\} $ such that $S_ i \cong T_{\sigma (i)}$ for all $i \in \{ 1, \ldots , n\} $. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $j$ be the smallest index such that $A_1 \subset B_ j$. Then the map $S_1 = A_1 \to B_ j/B_{j - 1} = T_ j$ is an isomorphism. Moreover, the object $A/A_1 = A_ n/A_1 = B_ m/A_1$ has the two filtrations 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \subset A_2/A_1 \subset A_3/A_1 \subset \ldots \subset A_ n/A_1  \]
</div>
    <p>
       and 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \subset (B_1 + A_1)/A_1 \subset \ldots \subset (B_{j - 1} + A_1)/A_1 = B_ j/A_1 \subset B_{j + 1}/A_1 \subset \ldots \subset B_ m/A_1  \]
</div>
    <p>
       We conclude by induction. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>